@startuml
' ============================================================================
' METADATA
'   author:       TheRobotDrafts Examples
'   description:  Complex Yourdon-style Data Flow Diagram for an ERP order-to-
'                 cash flow: a Level-0 DFD with 13 processes across sales,
'                 inventory, billing, and shipping, plus a zoomed Level-1
'                 explosion of the "Pick & Pack" process. Multiple shared data
'                 stores and external entities.
'   usecase:      Teaching large leveled DFDs: cross-functional process groups,
'                 stores shared across departments, numbering hierarchy (1, 2,
'                 ... then 6.1, 6.2 ...), and how a Level-1 zoom keeps balance
'                 with its parent Level-0 bubble.
'   complexity:   complex
'   domain:       ERP / order-to-cash
' ============================================================================

title Data Flow Diagram - Complex ERP (Level-0 + Level-1 zoom)

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' TEACHING NOTES
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' Large DFDs are organized by EXPLODING bubbles hierarchically:
'   Level-0  : major processes (1..N) across the whole system.
'   Level-1  : each level-0 bubble N explodes into N.1, N.2, ...
'   Level-2  : further explosion of any N.M into N.M.1, N.M.2 ...
'
' Each explosion must be BALANCED: the flows crossing the boundary of bubble N
' (in the level-0 diagram) must be the SAME flows crossing the boundary of the
' N.1/N.2 group (in the level-1 diagram). Data stores may appear/disappear
' internally but boundary flows are conserved.
'
' Stores are often SHARED across processes from different functional groups
' (e.g. the Orders store is read by Sales and written by Fulfillment). This is
' normal in an ERP — the store is the integration point.
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

' ============================
' LEVEL-0 DIAGRAM (13 processes)
' ============================
package "Level-0 — Order to Cash" {

  ' --- Externals ---
  rectangle "Customer"        as Cust <<externalEntity>>
  rectangle "Supplier"        as Sup  <<externalEntity>>
  rectangle "Bank"            as Bank <<externalEntity>>

  ' --- Sales group ---
  usecase "1\nReceive\nOrder"        as P1 <<process>>
  usecase "2\nCredit\nCheck"         as P2 <<process>>
  usecase "3\nPrice &\nTax"          as P3 <<process>>

  ' --- Inventory group ---
  usecase "4\nCheck\nStock"          as P4 <<process>>
  usecase "5\nReserve\nStock"        as P5 <<process>>

  ' --- Fulfillment group ---
  usecase "6\nPick &\nPack"          as P6 <<process>>
  usecase "7\nShip"                  as P7 <<process>>

  ' --- Billing group ---
  usecase "8\nInvoice"               as P8 <<process>>
  usecase "9\nApply\nPayment"        as P9 <<process>>

  ' --- Replenishment group ---
  usecase "10\nReorder"              as P10 <<process>>
  usecase "11\nReceive\nGoods"       as P11 <<process>>

  ' --- Reporting ---
  usecase "12\nUpdate\nSales Ledger" as P12 <<process>>
  usecase "13\nReport\nDashboards"   as P13 <<process>>

  ' --- Shared data stores ---
  storage "Orders\n(D1)"      as D1 <<dataStore>>
  storage "Inventory\n(D2)"   as D2 <<dataStore>>
  storage "Customers\n(D3)"   as D3 <<dataStore>>
  storage "Invoices\n(D4)"    as D4 <<dataStore>>
  storage "Shipments\n(D5)"   as D5 <<dataStore>>
  storage "Metrics\n(D6)"     as D6 <<dataStore>>

  ' --- Boundary flows ---
  Cust --> P1 : order
  P3   --> Cust : quote/confirm
  Sup  --> P11 : goods received
  P10  --> Sup  : purchase order
  Bank --> P9  : payment
  P13  --> Cust : statements

  ' --- Sales internal ---
  P1 --> D1 : order
  P1 --> P2 : order
  D3 --> P2 : credit profile
  P2 --> P3 : approved order
  P3 --> D1 : priced order

  ' --- Inventory ---
  D1 --> P4 : order lines
  D2 --> P4 : on-hand qty
  P4 --> P5 : availability
  P5 --> D2 : reservation

  ' --- Fulfillment ---
  D1 --> P6 : order
  D2 --> P6 : pick list
  P6 --> P7 : packed shipment
  P7 --> D5 : shipment record
  P7 --> Cust : delivery

  ' --- Billing ---
  D5 --> P8 : shipment
  P8 --> D4 : invoice
  D4 --> P9 : invoice
  P9 --> D4 : payment applied
  P9 --> Bank : deposit

  ' --- Replenishment ---
  D2 --> P10 : low stock
  P11 --> D2 : restock

  ' --- Reporting (reads many stores) ---
  D1 --> P12 : orders
  D4 --> P12 : invoices
  P12 --> D6 : ledger entry
  D6 --> P13 : metrics
}

' ============================
' LEVEL-1 ZOOM of bubble 6 (Pick & Pack)
' ============================
package "Level-1 — Explode bubble 6 (Pick & Pack)" {

  rectangle "Fulfillment\nClerk"        as Clerk <<externalEntity>>
  usecase "6.1\nGenerate\nPick List"    as P61 <<process>>
  usecase "6.2\nPick\nItems"            as P62 <<process>>
  usecase "6.3\nPack &\nLabel"          as P63 <<process>>
  usecase "6.4\nStage\nfor Ship"        as P64 <<process>>

  storage "Inventory\n(D2)"   as D2b <<dataStore>>
  storage "Pick Queue\n(D7)"  as D7  <<dataStore>>

  ' Boundary flows that must match bubble 6 in level-0:
  '   IN:  order (from D1), pick list (from D2)
  '   OUT: packed shipment (to P7)
  ' Here we re-express them at the finer grain.
  P61 --> D7  : pick ticket
  D2b --> P61 : stock location
  D7  --> P62 : pick ticket
  P62 --> P63 : picked items
  Clerk --> P62 : manual pick
  P63 --> P64 : packed parcel
  P64 --> P7b : packed shipment

  ' Forward-declare the same downstream process for the zoom boundary.
  usecase "7\nShip" as P7b <<process>>
}

note bottom of P6
  **Leveling & balance**
  Bubble 6 in Level-0 has inputs {order, pick list} and output
  {packed shipment}. Its Level-1 explosion (6.1..6.4) must carry
  those SAME flows across its boundary — only the INTERNAL detail
  (pick queue D7, clerk interaction) is new. If a flow is added or
  dropped, the diagrams are out of balance and must be reconciled.
end note

note right of D2
  **Shared stores**
  D2 (Inventory) is written by Reserve (5), Reorder (11), and read by
  Check Stock (4), Pick & Pack (6). Shared stores are the integration
  point between functional groups in an ERP — they are expected, not
  a smell.
end note
@enduml
